the devastation of Grenfell tower is  hidden from view today but the damage to  the ground on which it stands is  becoming clear an independent study has  found the fire contaminated the soil  with chemicals that could pose a grave  risk to public health for residents of  the Lancaster West Estate rebuilding  their lives nearly two years on it is  confirmation of their worst fears it's  really upsetting it's something that  should have been done from the start it  should have taken nearly two years to do  everyone's affected that live close  because you're always ill a lot of  people ended up coughing really badly I  have to do something now because they  finally got been poor they need to clear  up the soil and the area and there are  thousands of people who live here who've  been affected by this so that I mean  we're talking about huge public health  screening program and this could go on  for this will go on for years and years  and years  this is the source of the contamination  charred remnants of insulation boards  the researchers say gave off deadly  hydrogen cyanide during the fire and  then left potentially deadly chemicals  in the ground afterwards from our  analysis we found number of different  contaminants which we'll have in terms  they're categorized as respiratory  sensitizers that within the long  exposure they will lead potentially to  asthma as well even to a high risk of  getting cancer soil gathered within 200  meters of the tower contained up to 160  times the normal concentration of  cancer-causing chemicals and researchers  are calling for long term health  screening for residents and emergency  responders councillor judith Blakeman is  concerned her residents garden one of  scores in the area may have been  contaminated what worries me now when  even personally because I used to cook a  lot of rhubarb crumbles and my  neighbor's children loved them and  they've eaten them all I don't get  matters to me because I'm old but for  the children it's really worrying public  health England has been monitoring the  air quality around Granville Tower but  not the soil and they say they will  examine this report separate soil  samples are being analyzed by the  Environment Agency but for now the  health advice to locals remains the same  any produce grown around here should be  washed and peeled before being eaten and  anyone gardening should wear gloves a  government spokesman said they take  these findings seriously and that anyone  with health concerns should see their GP  Paul Kelso sky news
 

